{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For power: use P = VI when you know voltage and current; use P = I²R when you know current and resistance",
   "For energy bills: convert power to kW (divide watts by 1,000) and time to hours",
   "Energy (kWh) = power (kW) × time (hours)",
   "Cost = energy (kWh) × price per kWh"
  ],
  "title": "Electrical Power and Energy Bills",
  "content": "<p>Unit errors destroy marks in energy-bill calculations. Check at the start: power must be in <strong>kilowatts</strong>, time in <strong>hours</strong>.</p><p><strong>Power equations:</strong></p><ul><li>\\(P = VI\\) — use when you have voltage and current</li><li>\\(P = I^2R\\) — use when you have current and resistance</li><li>\\(E = QV\\) — energy transferred by a charge</li></ul><p><strong>Energy bills:</strong></p><ul><li>Energy (kWh) = power (kW) × time (hours)</li><li>Cost (p) = energy (kWh) × price per kWh (p)</li><li>1 kWh = 3,600,000 J</li></ul><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Using watts instead of kilowatts</li><li>Using minutes or seconds instead of hours</li><li>Forgetting to square current in P = I²R</li></ul>"
 },
